# This file starts by ignoring everything, then names what is public. A denylist
# fails open -- anything added later ships unless someone remembers to exclude
# it -- and git history cannot be unpublished. An allowlist fails closed instead:
# a new top-level path stays invisible until it is named here.
#
# Order matters: ignore all, allow the public paths, then re-ignore build output
# that lives inside them.
#
# **A new file at the repository root is invisible until it is named below**, and
# it fails silently: `git status` will not mention it, `git add` will refuse it
# with no message worth reading, and nothing else goes wrong. That is the
# allowlist working as designed, and it is also the only way it bites.
# `git check-ignore -v <path>` says which line is responsible.

/*

# The grammar, and the command line over it. `god-core` owns the verb set, the
# plan, the checking and every message; `god-cli` takes a pipeline and a column
# list and writes a query. The data never crosses that boundary — the host
# already holds the table and only lacks the query — so there is no bridge here
# and nothing large moves.
!/god-core/
!/god-cli/

# The parity harness: one corpus, one fixture, run in R and in Python and
# compared. This is the check the whole project rests on, and it is tracked
# because a claim nobody can re-run is not evidence.
!/parity/

# What the README draws. One SVG rather than a pair, because the diagram carries
# its own tinted ground and so reads on a light page and a dark one alike; a
# transparent drawing is what forces a second `-dark` copy. The book's own images
# live under `book/`, which is allowed further down, so this directory holds only
# what the front page needs.
!/images/

# The two bindings
!/r-pkg/
!/py-pkg/

# The manual. It stays with the engine because every table in it is computed by
# the engine at render time, so one engine change invalidates every page at once.
!/book/

!/.github/

# Root files
!/README.md
!/CHANGELOG.md
!/CONTRIBUTING.md
!/LICENSE
!/NOTICE
!/Cargo.toml
!/Cargo.lock
!/.gitattributes
!/.gitignore

# Local test-round reports, sitting beside `book/`. The `/*` above already hides
# the directory, so this line adds no enforcement. It is here to say the folder
# is deliberate rather than stray, and that it is never meant to ship -- worth
# writing down precisely because the allowlist makes every unnamed path look the
# same.
/test/

# Secrets
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key

# Rust
# `target/` is unanchored on purpose: `/target/` would miss a stray
# `god-core/target/` left by a subdirectory build.
target/
**/*.rs.bk

# Cargo.lock is committed -- god-cli is a binary crate, so the pinned versions
# are part of the build contract.

# Generated output
/output/

# Quarto
/book/_book/
/book/.quarto/
/book/site_libs/
/book/_freeze/
**/*.quarto_ipynb

# knitr leaves these beside the chapter it rendered. They arrived on 2026-08-07
# when the book moved from the jupyter engine to knitr, which it did so that one
# document could hold an R tab and a Python tab. The jupyter engine produced
# neither, so neither was named here, and the allowlist does not catch them: it
# un-ignores `/book/` wholesale, so anything generated *inside* the directory
# looks exactly like a chapter.
**/*.knit.md
**/*_files/

# R
.Rproj.user/
.Rhistory
.RData
.Rapp.history
.Ruserdata
*.Rproj
*.Rcheck/
r-pkg/*.tar.gz

# Produced during a build rather than written by hand: `configure` copies the
# engine into inst/bin/, and `.prepare` stages the crate sources into
# tools/rust/ so a subdirectory build can compile them.
r-pkg/god/inst/bin/
r-pkg/god/tools/rust/

# The same thing on the Python side: `setup.py` copies the engine into the
# package before the wheel is built. It was not named here until the hook that
# creates it existed, and a built engine is not something to commit.
py-pkg/god/god/bin/
py-pkg/god/build/
py-pkg/god/dist/
py-pkg/god/*.egg-info/

# Jupyter
.ipynb_checkpoints/
*.ipynb

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.venv/
venv/
book/.venv/
dist/
build/
*.whl
.pytest_cache/

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
.DS_Store?
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~
*.bak
*.orig
